75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (A process that touches a page missing from physical memory triggers a page fault. The operating system must then evict a resident page to make room. This choice is the page replacement problem. An evicted page that the process needs again causes another fault, and each fault costs a disk read, often several milliseconds on a spinning disk. A good policy keeps the pages a process will use soon.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
76 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Programs rarely touch memory at random. They reuse a small set of pages for a stretch of time, then move on to another set. This tendency is called locality of reference. Locality is the reason replacement policies can work at all. Policies differ in how well they exploit it, and this study measures that difference.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
77 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (The study compares three policies: First-In First-Out \(FIFO\), Least Recently Used \(LRU\) and Optimal \(OPT\). OPT evicts the page whose next use lies farthest in the future. It needs future knowledge, so no real system can run it. It serves as the lower bound against which the other two are judged.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
78 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (The sources below are classic works on virtual memory. The citations come from my own knowledge, not from a live database search during this session. Please verify each entry in the library catalogue or Google Scholar before submitting.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
79 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (1.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
80 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (L. A. Belady, "A Study of Replacement Algorithms for a Virtual-Storage Computer," IBM Systems Journal, vol. 5, no. 2, 1966. Belady introduced the OPT benchmark and reported that FIFO can fault more with more frames.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
81 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (2.P. J. Denning, "The Working Set Model for Program Behavior," Communications of the ACM, )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
82 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (vol. 11, no. 5, 1968. Denning formalised locality as a working set, the pages referenced within a recent time window. This model explains why a memory smaller than the working set thrashes.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (P. J. Denning, "Virtual Memory," ACM Computing Surveys, vol. 2, no. 3, 1970. This survey gives a readable overview of locality, replacement policies and thrashing.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
