268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Belady's anomaly. FIFO faults 9 times with 3 frames and 10 times with 4 frames on the test string. LRU and OPT never increase faults when frames are added, because they belong to the class of stack algorithms. I did not sweep the random patterns for anomalies, so I cannot say how often FIFO produces one in these workloads.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Answers to the sub-questions. \(1\) Yes, LRU beats FIFO whenever locality exists. \(2\) LRU stays closer to OPT than FIFO does, but the gap to OPT remains 9 to 10 points on the skewed workload at 16 frames. \(3\) FIFO can show the anomaly on some strings. \(4\) The phased 100% pattern stops faulting at about 8 frames, which equals its working set size.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (This study tested FIFO, LRU and OPT on five reference patterns, across five memory sizes and five seeds. LRU outperformed FIFO on every pattern with locality, and the gap was largest at moderate memory sizes. On uniform random references, the two policies were indistinguishable. The results support H1, H2, H3 and H4 on the patterns tested.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (For a system designer, the practical reading is that replacement policy matters most when memory is tight relative to the working set. When memory fits the working set, almost any reasonable policy performs well. When it does not, the choice of policy changes the fault rate by tens of percentage points. Real operating systems therefore approximate LRU with cheaper methods such as the clock algorithm, which tracks recent use with one reference bit per page instead of a full ordering.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (The workloads are synthetic. They do not come from real program traces, so the absolute fault rates should not be read as predictions for real applications.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
273 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (The page space is 100 pages and each run has 10,000 references. Larger spaces and longer traces could change the rates.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
274 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (The model ignores page size, dirty-page write-back costs, TLB effects and competition between processes. Each of these affects real replacement cost.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
275 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Phase length \(500\) and working set size \(8\) were fixed. Varying them would show how sensitive the results are to the locality parameters.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (Five seeds give a rough view of variance. More seeds would tighten the confidence intervals. Future work: run the same policies on recorded traces from real programs, add the clock algorithm to the comparison, and measure the working set size directly with Denning's window model.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
